{#
  T-7.06 — «تجارب عملائنا», the customer-stories feed.

  | Variable                        | Type    | Description                       |
  |---------------------------------|---------|-----------------------------------|
  | component.title                 | ?string | Section heading                   |
  | component.cta_label             | ?string | Falls back to the catalogue       |
  | component.cta_url               | ?string | No URL, no button                 |
  | component.items[].image         | string  | The photograph                    |
  | component.items[].alt           | ?string | Empty means decorative            |
  | component.items[].shape         | string  | square · portrait · tall          |
  | component.items[].brand         | ?string | Brand tag, first in the row       |
  | component.items[].tags          | ?string | Category tags, comma separated    |
  | component.items[].hotspot_x     | ?int    | 0–100, a PERCENTAGE               |
  | component.items[].hotspot_y     | ?int    | 0–100, a PERCENTAGE               |
  | component.items[].product_id    | ?string | Resolved at runtime, never stored |
  | position                        | int     | Sorting number, from zero         |

  THE SOURCE IS A THEME SETTING, BY RULING. The owner closed B6 on 2026-08-06:
  the blog is ruled out, and a story is an image with a brand tag, category tags
  and a hotspot — a shape the blog cannot carry and the customiser can.

  THIS IS A SECTION AND NOT A PAGE, AND THAT IS A PLATFORM FACT RATHER THAN A
  PREFERENCE. The backlog names `pages/stories/index.twig`, and no such route can
  exist: Salla's page set is fixed — the breadcrumb API inside the Twilight SDK
  enumerates it and `src/views/pages` mirrors it exactly — so a theme cannot mint
  one. What the artboard draws as a page therefore lands as the Home section the
  same artboard also draws. **Ruled by the owner on 2026-08-08 (OP-3):** the page
  gets its URL from a CMS page the merchant creates, named by a `stories_page_id`
  setting and rendered by `page-single.twig` — **which makes it T-7.01's**, along
  with the filter row and the brand dropdown. That task consumes the card and the
  grid below; it does not write a second feed.

  THE HOTSPOT FIELDS ARE STORED HERE AND RENDERED BY T-7.07, which has since
  landed: each card opens `components.stories.story-modal`, which hands the
  stored point to the T-4.06 primitive. One implementation, per the ruling.

  ONE HOTSPOT PER STORY, because `twilight.json` has no nested collection — the
  same limit T-4.06 hit and recorded. The ruling asks for "one or more"; one is
  what the schema can hold, and one is what the story artboard draws.
#}
{% set stories_items = component.items|default([]) %}
{% set stories_cta_url = component.cta_url|default('') %}

{% if stories_items|length %}
    {# The landmark is named by its own heading, and only when there is one: a
       dangling `aria-labelledby` is worse than an unnamed region. #}
    <section data-testid="store-home-stories"
             class="s-block stories container"
             {% if component.title %}aria-labelledby="stories-{{ position }}-title"{% endif %}>
        <div class="s-block__panel">
            {% if component.title %}
                {% include 'components.ui.section-header' with {
                    title: component.title,
                    align: 'start',
                    level: 2,
                    id: 'stories-' ~ position ~ '-title'
                } %}
            {% endif %}

            {#
              Masonry by CSS columns, which the criterion allows and a layout library
              does not. Columns also keep reading order and visual order the same
              thing: the browser balances the items down column one and then column
              two, which is the order they are written in.
            #}
            <ul class="stories__grid">
                {% for item in stories_items %}
                    {% include 'components.stories.story-card' with {
                        story: item,
                        id: 'story-' ~ position ~ '-' ~ loop.index
                    } only %}
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>

            {#
              T-7.07 — one story view per card, rendered here rather than inside the
              card because a `<dialog>` inside a `<li>` inside a masonry column would
              be laid out by that column. `showModal()` puts it in the top layer, so
              its position in the DOM has no bearing on where it appears; what it
              does affect is what it is nested in, and a list item is the wrong
              parent for a dialog.

              They cost almost nothing while closed: a closed dialog is
              `display: none`, so its lazy image is never fetched, and its hotspot is
              marked deferred so no product lookup fires until it opens.
            #}
            {% for item in stories_items %}
                {% include 'components.stories.story-modal' with {
                    story: item,
                    id: 'story-' ~ position ~ '-' ~ loop.index
                } only %}
            {% endfor %}

            {% if stories_cta_url %}
                {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                    label: component.cta_label|default(trans('theme.stories.follow_cta')),
                    href: stories_cta_url,
                    {# T-2.11 — the neutral outline is a variant now, not a
                       per-section override. See 04-components/buttons.scss. #}
                    variant: 'neutral',
                    class: 'stories__cta'
                } %}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </section>
{% endif %}
